.Dd $Mdocdate: September 22 2026 $
.Dt FUGUPASS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fugupass
.Nd FuguPass vault core
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Cm create
.Fl k Ar threshold
.Fl m Ar machine
.Fl r Ar rounds
.Op Fl p Ar slots
.Ar oracle ...
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Cm canary
.Ar oracle
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Cm ls
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Cm show
.Op Fl w
.Ar name
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Cm add
.Op Fl T Ar type
.Op Fl c Ar class
.Op Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Ar name
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Cm gen
.Op Fl T Ar type
.Op Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Ar name
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Cm totp
.Ar name
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Cm audit
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the core process of FuguPass.
It holds the vault, the one-shot subcommands, and the ceremonies.
One vault is one directory of files.
.Pp
.Nm
reads each passphrase from the terminal.
It runs each helper program as a child process, and text alone
crosses that boundary.
.Pp
A run with no subcommand starts an interactive session.
.Nm
opens the session, and it then spawns
.Xr fugupass-repl 1
as a child process, with a request pipe and a reply pipe.
That child reads each operator command line and shows the non-secret
output, and
.Nm
runs each command.
No secret crosses the two pipes.
.Pp
The session locks on the command
.Cm quit ,
at the end of the request pipe, and after an idle time with no
request.
The lock erases every session secret, and the closed reply pipe ends
the child.
The config file holds the idle time as
.Ic lock-timeout ,
and the default is 300 seconds.
.Pp
Every subcommand but
.Cm create
opens a session of the vault first.
The session reads the passphrase once.
It takes the quorum of
.Ar threshold
oracles from the reachable oracles, in the order of the config file.
It prefers the oracles that hold a live index wrap on this machine.
It then verifies the passphrase at the canary record of each quorum
oracle, one oracle at a time, and it stops at the first failure.
The same answers open the index of the vault.
.Pp
A reveal of one entry sends one request to each quorum oracle.
The session unmasks one share of each answer, it reconstructs the
key of the entry, and it opens the entry file of that key.
A failure at one oracle moves that oracle out of the quorum, and the
next reachable oracle takes the place.
Each reveal substitutes each reachable oracle at most once, and it
stops when no untried reachable oracle remains.
One record is one slot at one oracle, and one session sends two
requests at most to one record.
The third wrong request destroys the key material of that record at
the oracle.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d Ar directory
Take
.Ar directory
as the vault directory.
The default is
.Pa .fugupass
of the home directory.
.Nm
makes the directory when no directory of that path exists.
.El
.Pp
The subcommands are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Cm create
.Fl k Ar threshold
.Fl m Ar machine
.Fl r Ar rounds
.Op Fl p Ar slots
.Ar oracle ...
.Xc
Make a new vault on this machine.
The ceremony takes the master from a plate scan of
.Xr fugupass-scan 1 .
It writes the device factor and the config file, and it reads the
passphrase.
It then enrolls a canary record at each oracle, and it fills the
pool of slots.
Each slot takes one record at each oracle.
The ceremony ends with the index and the revocation kit.
.Pp
Each
.Ar oracle
argument holds one position of the ordered oracle set: the static
public key hex, one space, then the URL.
The first argument is position 1.
The three options below are mandatory:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl k Ar threshold
The count of oracles that one reveal needs.
The value must not exceed the count of the
.Ar oracle
arguments.
.It Fl m Ar machine
The name of this machine.
The name holds lowercase ASCII letters, digits, and hyphens, 1 to 64
bytes.
The config file and the revocation kit record it.
.It Fl r Ar rounds
The round count of
.Xr bcrypt_pbkdf 3 .
.El
.Pp
The option below is optional:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p Ar slots
The count of slots of the new pool.
The default is 64 slots.
The ceremony derives each slot, so a large pool takes a long
ceremony and many oracle requests.
The config file records the value as
.Ic pool-size .
.El
.Pp
The ceremony reads the passphrase twice, at the prompt
.Dq Passphrase:
and at the prompt
.Dq Passphrase again: .
The two reads must match.
No file of the vault verifies this passphrase, so the ceremony warns
before the first prompt.
.Pp
The ceremony writes these files of the vault directory: the device
factor, the config file, the counters file, one canary seal of each
oracle, and one index wrap of each oracle.
It writes one sealed file of each slot of the pool, one wrap of each
slot at each oracle, the index, and the revocation kit.
.Pp
The revocation kit is a plaintext file.
It names this machine, each oracle of the set, and the record file
name of each record of this machine at that oracle.
The kit holds no secret.
An operator of a self-hosted oracle needs a record file name to
delete that record.
The subcommand prints the path of the kit on the standard output.
.It Cm canary Ar oracle
Enroll the canary record of one oracle again.
.Ar oracle
is the position of that oracle in the ordered set.
The unlock of the session verifies the current passphrase first, so
a wiped canary of another oracle stops this subcommand.
The enrollment takes two reads of the passphrase, and the unlock
took the first one.
The subcommand therefore reads the same passphrase once more, and it
enrolls the record under a fresh canary mask.
It proves the fresh record with one immediate request, and it writes
the canary seal of that oracle.
.Pp
The re-enrollment kills this machine's index wrap of that oracle.
A session that holds the index key wraps the index share again at
once.
A session without that key deletes the dead wrap file, and it names
the provisioning ceremony.
.It Cm ls
Print the name of each entry of the index, one name on each line.
The subcommand reads the open index, and it sends no entry request.
.It Cm show Op Fl w Ar name
Reveal the entry
.Ar name .
The subcommand prints the secret of the entry to the terminal, and
it prints each metadata field to the standard output.
A metadata line holds the field name, a colon, one space, and the
value.
.Pp
The index holds the type of each entry, so a
.Cm show
of a mnemonic entry without the
.Fl w
option fails before the reveal.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl w
Print the words of a mnemonic entry.
.El
.It Xo
.Cm add
.Op Fl T Ar type
.Op Fl c Ar class
.Op Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Ar name
.Xc
Import a secret into the entry
.Ar name .
The subcommand reads the secret from the terminal, at the prompt
.Dq Secret: .
No argument and no environment variable carries a secret.
.Pp
A name that the index holds takes a rotation.
.Cm add
then seals the new secret in the slot of that entry, and it consumes
no slot of the pool.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl T Ar type
The type of the entry: password, mnemonic, passphrase, totp, note,
or shadow.
A new entry needs this option, and a rotation takes the type of the
entry from the index.
.It Fl c Ar class
The origin class of the entry: derived, stored, sovereign, or shadow.
The default is stored.
The type of the entry sets the classes that it takes, so a shadow
entry needs this option.
No file of the vault records the class, so the option reaches this
command alone.
.It Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
One metadata field of the entry.
The option can come more than one time, and each field name comes
one time only.
The type of the entry sets the field names that it takes.
.El
.It Xo
.Cm gen
.Op Fl T Ar type
.Op Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Ar name
.Xc
Make the derived entry
.Ar name
from a slot of the pool.
The entry takes the derived origin class, and the subcommand keeps
the candidate that the type needs.
.Pp
A name that the index holds takes a rotation.
.Cm gen
then consumes a new slot, it increments the version of the entry,
and it extends the slot list of the entry.
Each old version stays recoverable from the master plate.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl T Ar type
The type of the entry: password, mnemonic, or passphrase.
A new entry needs this option, and a rotation takes the type of the
entry from the index.
.It Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
One metadata field of the entry, as
.Cm add
takes it.
.El
.It Cm totp Ar name
Reveal the totp entry
.Ar name ,
compute the current code of it, and print the code to the terminal.
The computation runs on this machine, and it takes no request of its
own.
.It Cm audit
Print the state of the plate verification and of the shadow entries.
The first line holds
.Dq verified ,
a colon, one space, and the date of the last plate verification.
The subcommand then prints one line of each stale shadow entry: the
verification date of it, one space, and the entry name.
A shadow entry is stale when its verification date is older than the
audit age.
The config file holds that age as
.Ic audit-age ,
and the default is 365 days.
.Pp
The index holds the type of each entry, so the subcommand reveals
the shadow entries alone.
An entry of another type takes no oracle request.
.El
.Pp
.Cm add
and
.Cm gen
consume the lowest free slot of the pool that holds the wraps of
this machine at
.Ar threshold
live oracles or more.
Each one writes the index with the slot consumed, and it writes the
entry file after that.
The records and the wraps of the slot stay as they are.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width HOME
.It Ev HOME
The home directory of the default vault directory.
.El
.Sh FILES
The first path below is the default vault directory, and each other
path sits inside the vault directory.
.Bl -tag -width "machine/revocation-kit" -compact
.It Pa ~/.fugupass
the vault directory of a run without the
.Fl d
option
.It Pa index
the sealed index of the vault
.It Pa machine/canary.\& Ns Ar i
the canary check seal of oracle
.Ar i
.It Pa machine/config
the config file of this machine, plaintext
.It Pa machine/counters
the last counter of each record of this machine
.It Pa machine/factor
the device factor of this machine
.It Pa machine/revocation-kit
the revocation kit of this machine, plaintext
.It Pa machine/wrap.index.\& Ns Ar i
this machine's index wrap of oracle
.Ar i
.It Pa machine/wrap.\& Ns Ar e Ns Pa \&.\& Ns Ar i
this machine's wrap of slot
.Ar e
at oracle
.Ar i
.El
.Pp
The sealed file of one entry, and the sealed file of one free slot,
each take the lowercase hex of the hash of the slot key as the file
name.
A listing of the vault directory therefore names no entry.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits 0 on success, and 1 when a subcommand fails.
A session exits 0 when the interface process exited 0, and 1 on every
other end of it.
An unknown subcommand and a wrong command line give the usage on the
standard error and the exit status 2.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Every report goes to the standard error.
A report of a failed request names the oracle and one of the four
states below.
The four states take four different user actions.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It the oracle answers an HTTP error
The oracle counted no attempt, and the request is retryable.
.It the transport fails
The oracle can have counted the attempt, because the answer can get
lost after the record changes.
.It the answer fails the authentication of the oracle
The static public key of the config file does not match the oracle
that answered.
Check the provisioning of that position, and the transport to it.
.It the answer is junk
The oracle answers junk for a wrong passphrase, for a missing or
wiped record, and for a counter violation.
The response bytes hold the cause of none of them.
.El
.Pp
The reports below name a state of the session.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It the canary of oracle Ar i : the check fails
The passphrase does not open the canary of that oracle, and the
report names the URL of it.
A failure at the first canary of the session names the passphrase
and the record-side causes of that oracle, because the two look
identical.
A failure after a pass at another oracle names the record-side
causes alone.
The session sends no entry request to that oracle.
.It the quorum takes Ar k No reachable oracles, and Ar m No answered
Fewer than
.Ar k
oracles are reachable.
The report names the state of each oracle, and the session performs
no reveal.
.It the index does not open, and the canary of each quorum oracle passed
The index file or one index wrap of this machine is wrong.
This failure is no junk answer, and the report names both causes.
.It the index stays closed: the quorum covers fewer than Ar k No live index wraps
The report names each dead or unreachable index wrap, and it names
the provisioning ceremony.
The session opens no entry name until the wraps come back.
.It slot Ar e : the entry of this quorum does not open
The reconstructed key does not open the entry file.
The failing oracle has no name, because the decrypt is the only junk
detector.
The session substitutes one place of the quorum and tries again.
.It slot Ar e No at oracle Ar i : the session sent two requests to this record
A decrypt failure names no oracle, so each record of the quorum can
hold the wrong pin.
The third wrong request destroys the key material of that record at
the oracle.
The session therefore stops the reveal of that slot.
.It slot Ar e : the session reveals it no other way
No untried reachable oracle remains, so the substitutions end.
.It the pool holds Ar n No free slots, at the low watermark of Ar w
The refill ceremony adds slots to the pool.
.Cm add
and
.Cm gen
still run.
.It the pool of this vault holds no free slot
.Cm add
and
.Cm gen
refuse a new entry.
The refill ceremony adds slots to the pool.
.It no free slot of the pool holds the wraps of this machine at Ar k No live oracles
Free slots remain, and no one of them reveals on this machine.
The provisioning ceremony writes the wraps of this machine.
.El
